Keys in the physical model — primary, surrogate, foreign, and natural.

Read any physical star diagram, and you'll meet four key abbreviations tagged on the columns: P-K, S-K, F-K, and N-K. They're not four independent things — they're the roles that keys play once the star is built. And getting them straight is how you read a model at a glance.

So, the four roles. The surrogate key is a warehouse-generated integer, meaningless to the business, minted for each dimension row — customer key equals eleven-oh-one. The primary key is the column that uniquely identifies a row — and in a dimension, the primary key is the surrogate key, so you'll often see the tag written primary-key-bar-surrogate-key. The foreign key is a column on the fact that references a dimension's primary key — the fact's customer key is a foreign key. And the natural, or business, key is the source system's own identifier, kept as an attribute on the dimension for traceability — never the join key. Customer i-d equals C-four-four-seven-one.

Here's how they connect the star. The foreign key on the fact — customer key — points at the primary-key-slash-surrogate-key of dim customer. That single relationship, repeated once per dimension, is the star. Meanwhile the natural key, customer i-d, rides along inside the dimension, so you can always trace a warehouse row back to the operational system — but no fact ever joins on it.

Why split the surrogate from the natural key? This is modules two and four, made physical. The join runs on the surrogate — a fast integer, source-independent, and, crucially, able to carry slowly-changing history, since two versions of one customer share a customer i-d but get different customer keys. The natural key is retained purely as an attribute — the audit-trail home. So: four tags, one story. The fact's foreign key joins to the dimension's primary key, which is its surrogate key; and the natural key sits beside it for traceability. Join on surrogates; keep naturals as attributes.
